{
  "dom_id": "step-11066",
  "name": "Example: \n                    Finding a Trigonometric Ratio of an Acute Special Angle",
  "problem": "Evaluate $\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)$ by converting the angle to degrees.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we convert $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians to an equivalent measure in degrees. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi} 6\\right) \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = \\dfrac {180^\\circ} {6} = 30^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\sin 30^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFinally, since we know that $\\sin 30^\\circ = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left( \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)  = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
